### How does authentication work with OAuth2 and JWT?

A central identity provider authenticates the user and issues a signed JWT access token. Each service validates the token's signature and expiry locally without calling the identity provider on every request.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.http.SessionCreationPolicy;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;

@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable()) // Stateless API, no CSRF needed
            .sessionManagement(session ->
                session.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/v1/public/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers("/api/v1/transfers/**").hasRole("TELLER")
                .requestMatchers("/api/v1/accounts/**").hasAnyRole("TELLER", "CUSTOMER")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2.jwt(jwt -> {}));
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

```text
Authentication flow:
  1. Rajesh logs in -> identity provider verifies credentials
  2. Identity provider issues signed JWT with sub=rajesh, roles=[CUSTOMER]
  3. Rajesh sends JWT in Authorization: Bearer header
  4. Each service validates signature + expiry locally
  5. No server-side session needed -> stateless, scalable
```

### How does RBAC authorization work?

Roles ride inside the JWT as claims. Services enforce permissions with method-level annotations.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import org.springframework.security.access.prepost.PreAuthorize;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1")
public class SecureController {

    @GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
    @PreAuthorize("hasAnyRole('CUSTOMER', 'TELLER')")
    public String getAccount(@PathVariable String id) {
        return "Account details for " + id;
    }

    @PostMapping("/transfers")
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('TELLER')")
    public String createTransfer(@RequestBody TransferRequest request) {
        return "Transfer initiated by teller";
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/accounts/{id}")
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public String closeAccount(@PathVariable String id) {
        return "Account " + id + " closed by admin";
    }

    record TransferRequest(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {}
}
```

```text
RBAC enforcement:
  Rajesh (CUSTOMER): GET /accounts/SAV2001 -> 200 OK
  Rajesh (CUSTOMER): POST /transfers -> 403 Forbidden
  Priya (TELLER): POST /transfers -> 200 OK
  Pankaj (ADMIN): DELETE /accounts/SAV2001 -> 200 OK
```

### How does the API gateway provide edge security?

The gateway terminates TLS, validates the JWT, applies rate limiting, and routes to the correct service.

```yaml
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: account-route
          uri: lb://account-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/v1/accounts/**
          filters:
            - name: RequestRateLimiter
              args:
                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 50
                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 100
```

```text
Gateway security responsibilities:
  1. Terminate TLS (certificates managed in one place)
  2. Validate JWT once at the edge
  3. Rate limit: 50 req/sec sustained, 100 burst per user
  4. Route to the correct backend service
  5. Strip sensitive headers from external requests
```

### How does mutual TLS (mTLS) protect internal traffic?

With mTLS both the caller and the callee present certificates. A service only accepts calls from peers with valid identities.

```yaml
# Istio PeerAuthentication: enforce mTLS cluster-wide
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: default
  namespace: banking-prod
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT
```

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class MtlsExplanation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Without mTLS:");
        System.out.println("  Attacker inside network calls transfer-service directly");
        System.out.println("  Transfer-service accepts: request came from internal IP");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("With mTLS:");
        System.out.println("  Attacker has no valid certificate");
        System.out.println("  Transfer-service rejects: certificate validation failed");
        System.out.println("  Only services with mesh-issued certificates can communicate");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Without mTLS:
  Attacker inside network calls transfer-service directly
  Transfer-service accepts: request came from internal IP

With mTLS:
  Attacker has no valid certificate
  Transfer-service rejects: certificate validation failed
  Only services with mesh-issued certificates can communicate
```

### How does a service mesh enforce security transparently?

A mesh like Istio injects a sidecar proxy that handles mTLS, authorization policies, and observability without changing application code.

```yaml
# Istio AuthorizationPolicy: only account-service can call transfer-service
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: transfer-service-policy
  namespace: banking-prod
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: transfer-service
  rules:
    - from:
        - source:
            principals:
              - cluster.local/ns/banking-prod/sa/account-service
      to:
        - operation:
            methods: ["POST"]
            paths: ["/api/v1/transfers/*"]
```

```text
Service mesh security:
  - mTLS automatic between all services
  - Authorization policies restrict which service can call which
  - No application code changes required
  - Observability: every call logged with source/destination identity
```

### How does secrets management work?

Credentials stored in HashiCorp Vault or a cloud secrets manager, accessed with service identity, never in code.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class SecretsManagement {
    // WRONG: secrets in application.yml
    // spring.datasource.password=SuperSecretPassword123
    // -> If repo is leaked, password is exposed

    // CORRECT: secrets from Vault
    // spring.cloud.vault.uri=https://vault.nbi.internal:8200
    // spring.cloud.vault.authentication=KUBERNETES
    // -> Service authenticates with its Kubernetes service account
    // -> Vault returns the DB password, rotated automatically

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: password in config file -> leaked if repo exposed");
        System.out.println("CORRECT: password from Vault -> encrypted, access-controlled, rotated");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: password in config file -> leaked if repo exposed
CORRECT: password from Vault -> encrypted, access-controlled, rotated
```

### What is Zero Trust?

No request is trusted just because it came from inside the network. Every service authenticates and authorizes every request.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class ZeroTrust {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Zero Trust principles:");
        System.out.println("  1. Verify every request (even internal)");
        System.out.println("  2. Encrypt all traffic (mTLS everywhere)");
        System.out.println("  3. Least privilege (each service gets only needed access)");
        System.out.println("  4. Assume breach (monitor, log, alert on anomalies)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("In practice:");
        System.out.println("  Gateway validates JWT at edge");
        System.out.println("  Each service STILL validates JWT (does not trust gateway blindly)");
        System.out.println("  mTLS proves caller identity at transport level");
        System.out.println("  Authorization policy restricts caller-to-callee access");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Zero Trust principles:
  1. Verify every request (even internal)
  2. Encrypt all traffic (mTLS everywhere)
  3. Least privilege (each service gets only needed access)
  4. Assume breach (monitor, log, alert on anomalies)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Validate token issuer AND audience, keep tokens short-lived with refresh rotation, audit authorization decisions centrally, and return consistent 401/403.

```text
Senior security checklist:
  [x] OAuth2 + JWT for authentication
  [x] RBAC with roles in JWT claims
  [x] Gateway: TLS termination, JWT validation, rate limiting
  [x] mTLS for all service-to-service traffic
  [x] Service mesh authorization policies
  [x] Secrets in Vault, never in code
  [x] Zero Trust: every service validates every request
  [x] Token: validate iss, aud, exp; short-lived with refresh rotation
  [x] Audit: log every authorization decision centrally
```
